  "worked_solution": "Notice that our sentence can be re-phrased as \n\nFor any natural number $m,$ we have that ${100}^{m}$ is a perfect square.\n\nNow, \"${100}^{m}$ is a perfect square\" means that\n\nThere exists an integer number $n$ such that ${100}^{m}={n}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we get the following statement:\n\nFor any natural number $m,$ there exists an integer $n$ such that ${100}^{m}={n}^{2}.$\n\nMore formally, this reads as\n\n$\u2200\\,m\\in N,\\,\u2203\\,n\\in Z,\\,{100}^{m}={n}^{2}.$",
